,"credit":"","src":"https:\u002F\u002Fassets.bundesliga.com\u002Fcontender\u002F2026\u002F10\u002Fimago1085642951h.jpg?crop=845px%2C0px%2C4006px%2C3205px"}}],"meta":{"canonical":"grischa-promel-interview-my-heart-belongs-to-stuttgart","description":"Aged 31, Grischa Prömel got his long-awaited wish to play for hometown club VfB Stuttgart after making the switch from Hoffenheim in the summer. The German midfielder spoke to the Bundesliga about his road home, love of surfing and more...","keywords":[],"openGraph":{"description":"Aged 31, Grischa Prömel got his long-awaited wish to play for hometown club VfB Stuttgart after making the switch from Hoffenheim in the summer. The German midfielder spoke to the Bundesliga about his road home, love of surfing and more...","image":"https:\u002F\u002Fassets.bundesliga.com\u002Fcontender\u002F2026\u002F8\u002F2627_MD04_VFBBVB_CKB_169.jpg?crop=0px%2C465px%2C6826px%2C3847px","title":"Grischa Prömel: 'My heart belongs to VfB Stuttgart'","url":"https:\u002F\u002FTODO - we need the url structure!"},"title":"Grischa Prömel: 'My heart belongs to VfB Stuttgart'","x":{"card":"summary_large_image","creator":"@bundesliga_en","description":"Aged 31, Grischa Prömel got his long-awaited wish to play for hometown club VfB Stuttgart after making the switch from Hoffenheim in the summer. The German midfielder spoke to the Bundesliga about his road home, love of surfing and more...","image":"https:\u002F\u002Fassets.bundesliga.com\u002Fcontender\u002F2026\u002F8\u002F2627_MD04_VFBBVB_CKB_169.jpg?crop=0px%2C465px%2C6826px%2C3847px","site":"@bundesliga_en","title":"Grischa Prömel: 'My heart belongs to VfB Stuttgart'"}},"paragraphs":[{"type":"paragraph","body":["\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: More than four months have passed since your move to \u003Ca href=\"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.bundesliga.com\u002Fen\u002Fbundesliga\u002Fclubs\u002Fvfb-stuttgart\">VfB Stuttgart\u003C\u002Fa>. How have you settled in?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>\u003Ca href=\"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.bundesliga.com\u002Fen\u002Fplayer\u002Fgrischa-promel\">Grischa Prömel\u003C\u002Fa>:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"I've settled in very well. Of course, it was easy for me because I know the region and already have my social circle here. So I'm happy.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: What were the biggest difficulties in settling in, and what were the easiest things?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel: \u003C\u002Fstrong>\"Off the pitch, it was easy for me because I have a lot of friends here. My family still lives here, so that was pretty straightforward. On the pitch, you simply need time when you join a new team. You first have to get used to your teammates and understand the coach's playing system, and that simply takes time.\"\u003Cstrong>\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>"],"headline":""},{"type":"paragraph","body":["\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: Born in Bad Cannstatt, raised in Esslingen, and only joining the club you love at the age of 31. Describe to everyone out there what that means to you.\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"For me, of course, it's something very special. I was born here, grew up in Esslingen and spent large parts of my childhood and youth there. VfB is practically on your doorstep, and you follow the club. Even during my career, when I wasn't in the region, I always kept an eye on them. The fact that it finally worked out at 31, that I could move to VfB, is a special story.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: So you're the only player in the team who was born in Stuttgart?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"That could be. I haven't looked into it any further, but it could well be the case.\"\u003C\u002Fp>"],"headline":""},{"type":"paragraph","body":["\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: What were the decisive reasons for your move here?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"In the end, it was the overall package. On the one hand, of course, returning home and being close to my family. On the other hand, the sporting ambitions the club has and how the club has developed. The last few years have been extremely successful here, and I wanted to be part of that and help ensure that the success story continues.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: And then you scored your first goal for VfB in your very first home game. What memories and feelings do you have when you look back at the match against Cologne?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"It couldn't have gone any better. Everything just came together, really from the first minute to the last. Then the highlight was scoring the goal and celebrating in front of the \u003Cem>Cannstatter Kurve\u003C\u002Fem>. It's simply indescribable.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: Did something special develop between you and the fans at that moment, or was it simply because you had scored your first goal?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"Of course, it does something to you when you score your first goal for the club, in front of your home crowd. You run towards the Cannstatter Kurve, and Fritzle came towards me. I think I hugged him straight away. A lot goes through your head in that moment.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: At \u003Ca href=\"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.bundesliga.com\u002Fen\u002Fbundesliga\u002Fclubs\u002Ftsg-hoffenheim\">Hoffenheim\u003C\u002Fa>, you narrowly missed out on working with \u003Ca href=\"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.bundesliga.com\u002Fen\u002Fbundesliga\u002Fnews\u002Fsebastian-hoeness-who-is-the-bayern-munich-reserve-team-coach-stuttgart-12046\">Sebastian Hoeneß\u003C\u002Fa>. He left in 2022, and you arrived. Now you're together. What's your impression of him and his philosophy? How does it suit you, and how long does it take to get used to it?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"It's quite funny because now, on the second attempt, I've finally managed to work with Sebastian Hoeneß. I actually had conversations with him back then when it became clear that I was going to Hoffenheim. Then I arrived in Hoffenheim, and unfortunately he was no longer there. So, of course, I was looking forward to working with him now.\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\"You only have to look at VfB's development to know what an impact Sebastian Hoeneß has had on the club. It's incredible. That was also one of the reasons why I came here, because I saw how the individual players developed under him. That's important for the club, important for us as a team and for every player.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: What ambitions have you found here at the club compared to Hoffenheim or \u003Ca href=\"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.bundesliga.com\u002Fen\u002Fbundesliga\u002Fclubs\u002F1-fc-union-berlin\">Union Berlin?\u003C\u002Fa>\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"I always find it difficult to compare clubs. But you have to say that VfB have done an outstanding job over the last two or three years. Here in the city, or at the club, there's naturally a certain level of expectation. The team has played great football over the last few years, and that's what you're measured against now. And that's a good thing.\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\"Every player grows from that, and every player in our team wants to get the maximum out of their career. Of course, that only works if you stick together and are successful together.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Watch:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \u003Cem>Prömel's first Bundesliga goal for Stuttgart\u003C\u002Fem>\u003C\u002Fp>"],"headline":""},{"type":"paragraph","body":["\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: \u003Ca href=\"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.bundesliga.com\u002Fen\u002Fplayer\u002Fangelo-stiller\">Angelo Stiller\u003C\u002Fa> and \u003Ca href=\"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.bundesliga.com\u002Fen\u002Fplayer\u002Fatakan-karazor\">Atakan Karazor\u003C\u002Fa> were the established double pivot for years. Now a new competitor has arrived. How do you perceive the competition for places?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel: \u003C\u002Fstrong>\"I've said it a few times already. Competition is simply part of it. It's a fundamental part of professional football. Of course, everyone wants to play. I know that. I want to play, everyone gives their all, everyone tries to deliver their best performance, and in the end, the coach decides who plays. Nevertheless, we have a lot of good players in the squad, and there's competition not just in my position, but everywhere.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: Which of your qualities can this team benefit from? And can this team make you an even better player?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"Definitely. VfB will have had a reason for signing me. I simply try to leave my heart on the pitch and give everything in every game through my qualities, whether that's my leadership or my running ability. I always find it a bit difficult when you have to describe yourself and work out your own strengths. But I think I can help the team.\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\"As for how the team can help my game, at Hoffenheim I was in the box more often. Of course, we also played with one more central midfielder there, so I had a bit more freedom. That's why I have to adapt my game a little. But I think I'm getting better and better at that now.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: Nine games, 12 points. Rather a weak start to the season, and yet eighth place doesn't look too bad. Why have you struggled to get going?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"I think there are several factors. Fundamentally, we've progressed in the cup, we're doing quite well in the Champions League and in the Bundesliga I think we've now turned things around with the last two wins. Nevertheless, of course, the expectation is that we win games like those against \u003Ca href=\"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.bundesliga.com\u002Fen\u002Fbundesliga\u002Fclubs\u002Fhamburger-sv\">Hamburg\u003C\u002Fa> or \u003Ca href=\"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.bundesliga.com\u002Fen\u002Fbundesliga\u002Fclubs\u002Fborussia-moenchengladbach\">Gladbach\u003C\u002Fa>. But we simply didn't manage to perform consistently over 90 minutes and get the maximum out of ourselves.\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\"We have to get it into our heads that we have more quality. But everyone in the team knows that. We just have to show it, and if we manage that, we'll start putting together runs where we can celebrate a lot of wins again.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: You've played in every competitive match, most of them as a starter. Does that surprise you?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"No. Of course, I'm happy when I get a lot of playing time. I simply try to help the team, and if I can help the team and I'm on the team sheet at the weekend, then I'm happy, and hopefully the team is too.\"\u003C\u002Fp>"],"headline":""},{"type":"paragraph","body":["\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: How did you view Stuttgart from the outside during your time at Hoffenheim, and what major differences have you noticed now that you're experiencing the club from the inside?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"Pretty much the way I saw it from the outside. The footballing approach is there. We want to play bravely, we want to play directly forward, and at the same time be incredibly intense without the ball. That's supposed to be the identity of VfB. We probably haven't shown that often enough this season.\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\"There have been individual games where we've done it almost perfectly. But that's the benchmark you have to aim for, and you have to try to reach it as often as possible. It doesn't matter whether you're playing against Atlético [Madrid], [Werder] \u003Ca href=\"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.bundesliga.com\u002Fen\u002Fbundesliga\u002Fclubs\u002Fsv-werder-bremen\">Bremen\u003C\u002Fa> or Gladbach. In modern football, you simply have to invest, invest, invest, and then you get a lot back.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: Your professional Bundesliga career came about almost through an alternative route: through the second division with Union Berlin, promotion to the Bundesliga, the move to Hoffenheim, and now VfB Stuttgart and even the UEFA Champions League. How has that journey shaped you?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"I think that journey was immensely important for me, simply to experience everything along the way. I was always someone who wanted to compete with the best. Maybe I needed those steps, but it also shows that you never stop learning and can always continue to develop. It has nothing to do with age. You can still take steps forward. That's why I'm happy with the path I've taken.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: What are the special little details you can take away from playing in the Champions League?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"To some extent, it's surreal. I still remember when we were promoted to the Bundesliga with Union Berlin. Back then, the Champions League seemed so far away, and now you've worked towards that dream yourself. It's simply wonderful. Of course, the Champions League is the highest competition in club football. The best teams face each other there, and accordingly, everything has to work perfectly. You have less and less time, you're under more pressure, you have to be even more precise. I'm happy to have reached that level and to be able to compete with the best now.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: The guys have been used to playing in three competitions for years, sometimes even four with the national team. Is that something new that you have to learn in terms of your approach, recovery, nutrition and managing stress?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel: \u003C\u002Fstrong>\"Fundamentally, I also played internationally with Union Berlin and at Hoffenheim. Nevertheless, of course, it's a privilege to be involved in three competitions now. You really have to plan time for that. It's not something that just happens easily. You have to invest in regeneration, recovery and sleep, and then you can manage it.\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\"I think everyone has their own approach. For me, it helps to sleep a lot and recover. But I think every player dreams of this. Nobody really wants training sessions. Everyone wants competition, everyone wants a lot of playing time, and that's why the rhythm is pretty cool.\"\u003C\u002Fp>"],"headline":""},{"type":"paragraph","body":["\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: How do you recover best? How do you switch off in your free time?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel: \u003C\u002Fstrong>\"Switching off in the sense of not thinking about football and focusing on other things is naturally easy for me here because I have a lot of friends who aren't involved in football and who talk about other things.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: There's one title on your CV: U19 Bundesliga champion in 2014 with Hoffenheim. Is your move to VfB also connected to the ambition of winning titles?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"Of course, my goal is to be as successful as possible. The guys also talk a little about the time when they won the DFB Cup, what that meant to them and how it changed the way people perceived them. Of course, it would be a dream, but a lot of things have to go right for that to happen. You first have to take it game by game to get as far as possible.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: Schalke away, Frankfurt at home and Elversberg away are your next three Bundesliga opponents. You've won your last two games. What have you set out to achieve heading into December to ensure a stable first half of the season?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel: \u003C\u002Fstrong>\"We can still do a lot of things better, and we still have potential in the team that we simply have to show. I think the last two wins were important to get back on track, to climb the table a little and to make sure the pressure isn't quite so high. Nevertheless, I think we're well advised to assess each game individually.\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\"Now Schalke are coming up, and that won't be easy either. You don't get anything for free in the Bundesliga, no matter who the opponent is. And as I just said, every player still has potential, still has room for improvement, whether that's with or without the ball. There have been phases where we've created a lot of chances but haven't been effective enough. There have also been phases where we've perhaps been a little too open.\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\"Finding that balance is also something that concerns me as a central midfielder, bringing more balance into the team. But we definitely still have some work to do there.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: A record of 15 goals conceded and 15 scored don't quite reflect the full potential of the squad. In terms of balance and stability, how much do you personally need to contribute so that the team can improve collectively, not just individually?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"In the end, the whole team benefits when we have good balance and stability, and of course that concerns me too. I've already spoken to the video analysts about positioning because I'm the type of player who naturally likes to get into the box. I was very successful doing that at Hoffenheim. I scored a lot of goals.\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\"Now, of course, it's a different situation where you have to adapt a little. I'm continuing to work on that and trying to improve. Then we'll all have more fun if we don't get caught out by so many counter-attacks.\"\u003C\u002Fp>"],"headline":""},{"type":"paragraph","body":["\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: In your particular case, do you have to weigh things up during a game and recognise the right moment to join the attack and when to stay back?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"Of course. The coach often points out to me that in certain situations it might be better if I stay back to provide cover. Then, when we've pinned an opponent back, moved further up the pitch and switched play out wide, I can make occasional runs into the box again. But it's all a matter of coordination, depending on who you're playing alongside in that position. It takes time.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: Finally, something a little more personal. I've read that you enjoy travelling. What impresses you the most about it? What fascinates you the most?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"The different countries and cultures. I really like seeing a lot of the world. I enjoy being in nature, and I'm a huge fan of surfing. So you have to travel a bit to find the best waves. Over the last few years, I've spent a lot of time in South America, Brazil, Colombia, Mexico, Costa Rica, Nicaragua. That region was really beautiful.\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\"But I have two brothers who are also passionate surfers and have a bit more time available, and they always give me good tips. So before the summer break, I always call my brothers and ask what they'd recommend for that year, and they usually come up with some good travel suggestions.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: When did your interest in surfing develop?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel:\u003C\u002Fstrong> \"Pretty much from a young age. My parents wanted to emigrate to the USA back then and took us over there. We were still extremely young, and then they realised it wasn't quite so easy with three boys in the USA. So they abandoned the plan and returned to Germany.\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\"But during the time we were over there, my aunt lived in the USA, and they naturally developed so many friendships that throughout our youth, we basically always planned our summer holidays in the USA. Accordingly, we spent a lot of time on the water. My older brother and my younger brother also studied and went to school in the USA for a while, so we have friends there whom we like to visit.\"\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Bundesliga: You played for the Blues, \u003Ca href=\"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.bundesliga.com\u002Fen\u002Fbundesliga\u002Fnews\u002Fstuttgarter-kickers-club-by-club-historical-guide-klinsmann-24286\">Stuttgarter Kickers\u003C\u002Fa>, in your youth, and now you're with the Reds at VfB Stuttgart. Do you have a place in your heart for both clubs?\u003C\u002Fstrong>\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\u003Cstrong>Prömel: \u003C\u002Fstrong>\"No, I wouldn't say that. My heart has always belonged to VfB. Nevertheless, the Kickers were incredibly important for my development because I was able to take some good steps there. They supported me, and they probably played a big part in my career because that time was really good for me.\u003C\u002Fp>\n\u003Cp>\"I then moved from Kickers to Hoffenheim, and that's when my career really started to take off. So I'm very, very grateful to Kickers, but my heart belongs to VfB.\"\u003C\u002Fp>"],"headline":""}],"players":["DFL-OBJ-0025J3"],"publishe